  "question": {
    "text": "The work function of a substance is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>3</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>0</mn><mo> </mo><mi>eV</mi></math>. The longest wavelength of light that can cause the emission of photoelectrons from this substance is approximately:"
  },
  "options": [
    {
      "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>215</mn><mo> </mo><mi>nm</mi></math>",
      "is_correct": false
    },
    {
      "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>414</mn><mo> </mo><mi>nm</mi></math>",
      "is_correct": true
    },
    {
      "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>400</mn><mo> </mo><mi>nm</mi></math>",
      "is_correct": false
    },
    {
      "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>200</mn><mo> </mo><mi>nm</mi></math>",
      "is_correct": false
    }
  ],
  "correct_answer": [
    "B"
  ],
  "solution": {
    "text": "<p>For longest wavelength, the emitted electron will have nearly zero kinetic energy.</p><p>Therefore,</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>λ</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>h</mi><mi>c</mi></mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>e</mi></msub></mfrac></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>⇒</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1240</mn><mo> </mo><mi>nm</mi><mo>-</mo><mi>eV</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo> </mo><mi>eV</mi></mrow></mfrac></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>⇒</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>413</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>33</mn><mo> </mo><mi>nm</mi></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>⇒</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><mo>≈</mo><mn>414</mn><mo> </mo><mi>nm</mi></math></p>"
  }
